    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The treatment of melanoma has seen significant advances recently with the introduction of immunotherapies such as immune checkpoint inhibitors or dendritic cell vaccines. Dendritic cells (DCs) are crucial for successful immunotherapy due to their ability to initiate and modulate T-cell responses. Current research suggests that combining immunotherapies with targeted radiotherapy could be highly effective treatment strategies for melanoma. However, recent research has also indicated that ionizing radiation causes immunosuppression by disproportionately inducing cell death in immunogenic dendritic cell subsets that generate an immune response. This effect was not observed in tolerogenic dendritic cell subsets, which produce immunological tolerance. This project extended the existing knowledge by examining the effect of targeted radiotherapy on DC subsets localized in the tumor microenvironment. Our ultimate goal is to determine whether radiotherapy can impair or amplify DC function. To achieve that goal, DC subset numbers were evaluated using flow cytometry at different time points after targeted radiotherapy in two tumor mouse models, i.e., a transplantation tumor model and a genetically engineered mouse model. The genetically engineered mouse model can be chemically induced to develop melanomas, allowing for the exploration of melanomas developed from the mouse’s own cells. Our results show a transient decrease in the overall number of leukocytes. Interestingly, the number of immunogenic DCs, but not tolerogenic DCs, was rapidly decreased after radiotherapy but return to normal levels after 3 days. These results were consistent both in the transplantable model and in the genetically engineered mouse model, suggesting that  both models could be used for subsequent studies combining radiotherapy and immunotherapy strategies. Importantly, our results suggest that combinatory immunotherapeutic strategies should be administered at least 72 hrs after radiotherapy when immunogenic DC subsets numbers are back to normal levels.  Altogether, our comprehensive characterization of immune responses and DC subsets during radiotherapy allows for the rational design of combinatory therapeutic strategies.",
